<h2>Delivery planning starts with the model scope</h2>
<p>During <a href="/model-making-process">project scoping</a>, we review:</p>
<ul>
<li>Final model dimensions</li>
<li>Base, stand, and display-case requirements</li>
<li>Delivery destination</li>
<li>Transportation limits</li>
<li>Door and elevator dimensions</li>
<li>Stairs, corridors, and loading access</li>
<li>Available power</li>
<li>Handling requirements</li>
<li>Final placement and installation responsibilities</li>
</ul>
<p>Identifying these constraints early can prevent the finished model from becoming difficult to transport or install.</p>

<h2>When should a model be divided into sections?</h2>
<p>Large models may be <a href="/faq">designed in sections</a> when they cannot be safely transported or moved into the final space as one piece.</p>
<p>Sectional construction may be required because of:</p>
<ul>
<li>Shipping dimensions</li>
<li>Model weight</li>
<li>Door or elevator limits</li>
<li>Limited site access</li>
<li>Handling requirements</li>
<li>Future relocation</li>
<li>Phased development</li>
<li>Removable or interchangeable content</li>
</ul>
<p>The joints, alignment, electrical connections, base, and final assembly method must be coordinated as part of the model design.</p>

<h2>How is a model protected?</h2>
<p>Packaging depends on the model's size, fragility, destination, and intended handling.</p>
<p>Depending on the project, protection may include:</p>
<ul>
<li>A fitted transport case</li>
<li>Protective foam and internal supports</li>
<li>A custom shipping crate</li>
<li>Removable covers</li>
<li>Secured model sections</li>
<li>Separate packaging for delicate components</li>
<li>Handling and unpacking instructions</li>
</ul>
<p>The objective is to prevent movement, vibration, pressure, dust, and accidental contact during transportation.</p>

<h2>How is the delivery method selected?</h2>
<p>The appropriate method depends on the size of the model, destination, schedule, and level of handling required.</p>
<p>Options may include parcel delivery, freight, dedicated transportation, or coordinated on-site delivery. Fragile or complex models may require special handling rather than a standard shipping service.</p>
<p>The delivery plan should also account for the time required to unpack, assemble, connect, inspect, and position the model.</p>

<h2>What happens during installation?</h2>
<p>Depending on the project, installation can include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Unpacking and inspection</li>
<li>Assembly of model sections</li>
<li>Placement on a stand or display base</li>
<li>Installation of a protective cover</li>
<li>Alignment and final adjustment</li>
<li>Lighting and power connections</li>
<li>Functional testing</li>
<li>Removal of packaging materials</li>
</ul>
<p>The site should be ready before the model arrives, with appropriate access, display support, power, and clear responsibility for final approval.</p>

<h2>What should the client provide?</h2>
<p>Before production is finalized, provide:</p>
<ul>
<li>Delivery address and site contact</li>
<li>Required delivery date</li>
<li>Loading and parking information</li>
<li>Door, elevator, and corridor dimensions</li>
<li>Stairs or other access restrictions</li>
<li>Final display dimensions</li>
<li>Available power</li>
<li>Building-access hours</li>
<li>Installation and insurance requirements</li>
</ul>
<p>Photographs or a short video of the delivery route can also be useful for complex installations.</p>
